            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new word: what did saṁvatsaraḥ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: रविः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "रविः (raviḥ) — \"the sun\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "रविः (raviḥ) is the sun: the disc itself, not the light it sends."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have आकाशः for the open sky and चन्द्रः for the moon. रविः (raviḥ) is the third thing overhead, and the one the other two are measured against."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanskrit keeps several sun-words and this one has no agreed cousin outside India, so its history stops at the border. Its life since then has not: Hindi still calls Sunday ravivār, so the word gets said once a week by people who have never studied it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The third thing overhead, beside the sky and the moon."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
