            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the last word of the chapter: what did prakāśaḥ mean, and what did chāyā mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: तापः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "तापः (tāpaḥ) — \"heat\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "तापः (tāpaḥ) is heat: the warmth of the sun on a back, and by an easy stretch the heat of trouble."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It stands on tap, \"to be hot, to burn\" — the same root under tapas, the heat a person is said to build up by hard practice. Sanskrit treats effort and warmth as one thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin tepēre, \"to be warm\", is that root gone west, and English took tepid straight from it. Ends in -अः."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five things about light and warmth: the sun, a ray, light, shade, heat."
            }
          ]
        },
